{"name":"Mushroom Forest","hex":"#8e8062","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mushroom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the fleshy fruiting bodies of fungi typically produced above ground on soil or on their food sources (such as decaying wood).","A fungus producing such fruiting bodies."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having characteristics like those of a mushroom, for example in shape or appearance, speed of growth, or texture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mushroom"},{"term":"forest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dense uncultivated tract of trees and undergrowth, larger than woods.","Any dense collection or amount."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover an area with trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/forest"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mushroom","blocks":[{"text":"A mushroom is the fleshy, spore-bearing fruiting body of a fungus, typically produced above ground on soil or another food source. A toadstool generally refers to a poisonous mushroom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Sparrige_Sch%C3%BCppling_%28Pholiota_squarrosa%29.jpg/330px-Sparrige_Sch%C3%BCppling_%28Pholiota_squarrosa%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mushroom","wikidata":"Q83093"},{"title":"Forest","blocks":[{"text":"A forest is an ecosystem characterized by a dense community of trees. Hundreds of definitions of forest are used throughout the world, incorporating factors such as tree density, tree height, land use, legal standing, and ecological function. The United Nations' Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines a forest as, \"Land spanning more than 0.5 hectares with trees higher than 5 meters and a canopy cover of more than 10 percent, or trees able to reach these thresholds in situ. It does not include land that is predominantly under agricultural or urban use.\" Using this definition, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2025 found that forests covered 4.14 billion hectares, or approximately 31 percent of the world's land area in 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest","wikidata":"Q4421"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg/500px-Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg/500px-Zwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Amanita muscaria, the most easily recognised \"toadstool\", is frequently depicted in fairy stories and on greeting cards. It is often associated with gnomes.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZwerg_Postkarte_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png/500px-Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png/1280px-Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png/500px-Mushroom_cap_morphology2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Morphological characteristics of the caps of mushrooms","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMushroom_cap_morphology2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Hypomyces_lactifluorum_169126.jpg/500px-Hypomyces_lactifluorum_169126.jpg?utm_source=en.